  "query_preview": "/Sunday through Thursday/... \nThe relative pause in convective activity should be fairly short \nlived as the high will eventually become parked over the Four \nCorners, inducing southeasterly flow over the region which should \nprovide renewed moisture flux. PoPs have fallen slightly compared to \n24 hours ago, mainly for lower elevation areas, but that is not much \nof a surprise as it make take a little bit more time to get moisture \nprofiles back to more favorable levels. The more favorable spots to \nsee convection will once again be the High County and Southeast \nArizona, but downstream effects from any activity that does form\ncould have impacts on our lower elevation zones. Once we get \ncloser, and get inside the hi-res window, we will have a better \nidea on what impacts to expect. \n\nDaily chances for shower and thunderstorms will reach into the front \nhalf of the next workweek as sufficient moisture stays in place. The \nlower deserts, if not by Sunday, may finally get in on some of the \naction as low-level moisture looks to improve with model soundings \nindicating mixing ratios reaching 11-12 g/kg. That will aid in \nincreasing instability and limiting the cap that has been so \nprevalent the past few days. As typical with the monsoon, how one \nday plays out will heavily influence what happens the subsequent \nday, so day-to-day forecast changes should be expected, but with \nmoisture levels not likely to fall off through at least the middle \npart of the week, we may be looking at one of the more active \nperiods we have seen so far this summer.\n\nTemperatures will remain several degrees above seasonal levels \nthrough Sunday, but there appears to be at least some relief on the \nhorizon. With increased moisture, storm coverage, and cloud cover, \nafternoon highs over South-Central Arizona are expected to fall much \ncloser to normal for this time of year with projected lower-desert \nhighs between 101-106 degrees by Tuesday. Areas further displaced \nfrom the storm activity, namely Southwest Arizona and Southeast \nCalifornia, will see temperatures gradually decrease too thanks to a \nweakening trend with the prevailing high, but readings should remain \nslightly higher relative to locations further east. Highs for those \nwestern desert locations will be closer to 105-110 degrees through \nat least Thursday.",
